        "title_top": "",
        "title": "Expert Backed Review Summary",
        "text": "<p>Capstar is designed around one primary goal: killing adult fleas quickly. Its active ingredient, <strong>nitenpyram</strong>, belongs to the neonicotinoid class and acts on the flea nervous system. Unlike topical treatments that need to spread across the skin, Capstar is administered orally, either directly or hidden in a small amount of food. Speed is the product's biggest advantage. According to the official U.S. label, Capstar begins working within <strong>30 minutes</strong>, and studies found greater than 90% effectiveness against adult fleas on cats within six hours. This makes it particularly useful when a pet already has visible adult fleas and fast knockdown is the priority. For cats weighing <strong>2–25 pounds</strong>, the labeled dose is one 11.4 mg tablet. If the cat becomes reinfested, another dose can be administered as often as once per day. The product is intended for cats and kittens that are at least four weeks old and weigh at least two pounds. One important limitation is that Capstar is primarily a short-term adult-flea treatment rather than a persistent preventive. It kills fleas currently on the animal, but reinfestation can occur as new fleas emerge from the environment. Effective flea management may therefore require treating other infested pets and addressing immature flea stages in the home with an appropriate longer-term strategy. </p><p></p>",
        "body_align": "center",
        "content_width": "narrow",
        "align-heading": "center",
